ry  tales of this series which is that the  scale can actually be a dangerous tool  for some particularly for goal oriented  people now throughout the series we see  will getting on the scale regularly and  sometimes the number isn't what he wants  to see like so many people he expects to  see linear weight loss every week to  meet his 20 pound in 20 week goal but as  will's journey demonstrates nothing  about weight loss is straightforward or  simple the challenge is that will is  highly goal oriented he sees what he  wants then he goes out and gets it but  weight is not necessarily something that  we can precisely control and what  happens when someone who is highly  motivated by meeting their goals doesn't  actually meet them or as will's  psychologist puts it how the heck does  this guy cope  when he's not winning well it's not only  frustrating but it can actually trigger  some potentially destructive behaviors  when he doesn't lose his nice round  weekly pound i've been doing this stupid  crap and  i don't care what i weigh are you  feeling this morning well low energy did  you react to it in terms of the amount  of food that you ate this week i had two  days worth of reaction boiled eggs raw  vegetables that's it and i was doing  like 14 hours of fast  yeah  so this week is going to be ridiculous  wake up weigh in run right down then i'm  into the bag my first workout and a  second workout do a finisher just to  blow it out i've been doing the writing  session it's 4 27 i am going running  and then going to shoot things training  i only got four hours and 28 minutes of  sleep  will is motivated as  and goal-oriented people like this will  fight as long as they possibly can to  get where they want to be but even  motivated people lose their drive when  their source of validation which in  will's case is the scale becomes  invalidating and as his psychologist  says the more a person is stretched  sooner or later they snap  yep they snap they burn out they get  injured as he did at one point in the  journey or they just throw in the towel  and completely quit i don't want to do  any of this  i'm finished with  uh this project in my mind we blew it  we missed it it's over  and we got to stop and i'm ready to stop  giving up on a goal is usually what  happens when the goal is not entirely  within our control which as you can  imagine is a tough loss for us  goal-oriented people and in this case  where will's body weight chooses to land  and at what pace is not something that  he has direct control over regardless of  his commitment to his diet and fitness  plan and this brings me to my next big  takeaway message here which is that  perfectionist all or nothing thinking is  often our worst enemy when it comes to  changing hard habits i have to admit  that watching this series was both  triggering and therapeutic to me because  personally i saw a lot of myself in will  i have struggled with perfectionist  tendencies my whole life and like will i  feel like this has contributed to some  of my successes but also created major  barriers to life success because it's  created an anxiety that has at times  prevented me from taking risks what i  see and will is a relentless drive to go  all in and to run to the top of the hill  with that [ __ ] flag no matter what  and while this mentality is admirable in  some cases and is undoubtedly an asset  to his career success it isn't without  sacrifice now from listening to will's  own storytelling in this series it's  apparent that this all or nothing  mentality began at a very young age i  was on a tear the biggest winning streak  in hollywood history i noticed that most  people came back from christmas vacation  heavier and out of shape so the holidays  for me became an opportunity to extend  my lead i made it a point to come back  every new year in better shape than i  left last i would work out and sometimes  even abstain from christmas dinner as an  act of personal discipline so these 