cific to will i actually  think he seems to be doing a really  great job acknowledging his own triggers  and emotional limitations but it's worth  noting that perfectionist traits are  more common in folks with eating  disorders or disordered eating compared  to the general population so this is one  reason why i think extreme diet  challenges like these do require a real  risk benefit analysis here's what i've  learned about perfectionism it can often  keep you locked in a rigid mindset and a  behavior pattern that maybe doesn't  actually serve you very well in the long  run you spend so much time trying to be  perfect that it kills authenticity and  leaves no room for development growth or  even failure and will himself has  previously said that failure is  essential for success so it sounds like  he's taking a page out of his own book  by reframing his goal and focusing  instead on authenticity  because i can do anything  when i you know  tap into that soldier  right so it's not about the  yeah harsh energy it's hard yeah a more  sustainable mindset when i was in  university and beating myself up over  getting an a minus over an a plus one of  the most impactful things that my father  who was a professor ever said to me  about my perfectionism was abby if you  put yourself on a pedestal like this  you'll have nowhere left to grow and  he's totally right so i feel like will  is starting to realize that  perfectionism is no longer necessarily a  strength because in reality it sometimes  reduces life down to a very boring  series of emotions with very little  purpose or pleasure so ultimately what  is my advice  i say the goal here needs to change  if will was my client i would probably  tell him to put the weight loss  challenge on the back burner i don't  think it's actually helping him write  the book and i also don't think it's  going to net out any positive health  outcome either my focus instead would be  twofold one to focus on giving himself  some mental space for healing as he  unearths potentially painful memories  the weight gain or weight loss right now  is such a distraction from everything  else going on it's just so not about the  food and from what i can see i also  think he kind of knows this 202  flat  would be on schedule  your expert opinion mr ferguson does  that have anything to do with my age  or more to do with my food selection  i think it's got a little bit to do with  your brain it's not about the weight  or  the book  as much as what it says about my mind my  mind is suffering and subject to the  same appetites and desires and cravings  and self-destructive behavior as  everybody else and his team also seems  to know that putting the weight loss on  the back burner is in his best interest  as well even if at face value it may be  read as like a cop out or a failure will  quitting the show  may look like a failure to a lot of  people but there's actually another way  to look at it as  a breakthrough will is choosing himself  he's not hiding behind humor or control  or the idea of winning instead he's  giving himself space to process and deal  with his past someone's taking a step  back and not performing not producing  not succeeding in the conventional sense  is the best evidence of life progress so  my second suggestion on this is that  when and if will does decide to revisit  the food and exercise piece i would  recommend shifting the focus from any  kind of end goal towards the process in  other words rather than being goal  oriented to shift to being process  oriented and i think will actually comes  to this realization himself when he  realized that the structure of this kind  of high production weight loss series  isn't actually serving him it's not  helping his mental health it's not  helping his physical health and it's not  even helping him reach the weight goal  like don't  tell me about the schedule i don't want  the show or something like that mindset  is disruptive to me in the creative  process further to that he comes to  realize that the weight loss goal or  book writing goal isn't actually helpful  at all and the process and what he  learns from it is actually the most  valuable piece sometimes achieving the  actual goal earns you almost nothing but  the journey itself  teaches you everything you need to know  being process oriented towards this  weight loss journey is similar to  applying the intuitive eating concept of  data collection losing 20 pounds  instantly wouldn't really teach us  anything about nutrition or our bodies  if it was just to happen to us but if we  were to focus on the process we would  have a lot more motivation to maintain  the behaviors that serve us and to let  go of those that don't  so asking ourselves questions like how  does cutting 500 calories make us feel  how do we feel after our second workout  of the day do we binge on the weekends  are we cranky all the time is our sleep  affected do we feel energized how's our  digestion can we eat like this forever  or are we just going to crash binge or  become very unhappy people this is the  introspective information that really  helps shape our behaviors with better  awareness in the long term i also think  that when we set numeric weight loss  goals like 20 pounds and 20 weeks that  we lose sight of the forest from the  trees or in a metaphor used by will  himself we get lost worrying about the  wall  rather than each individual brick in the  series will talks about how his father  made his brother in him build a wall as  a project and he always used to feel so  defeated looking at this huge wall that  he needed to build but his father would  say forget about the wall worry about  that one brick it's the same thing with  weight loss the wall is the weight loss  goal and often it can become really  distracting when we see this big gaping  hole that we know we need to fill and we  don't know where to start  start with what you can manage every day  start with what you can actually control  what can we accomplish that we have  control over so that could mean i'm  gonna work out three days this week or  i'm gonna have salad with my lunch four  days this week let's just focus on that  goal and when we meet it we reevaluate  it to continue or add on i think the  will smith series actually paints a  pretty authentic and nuanced look at the  intersection of one's relationship with  food and body and everything else going  on in their life and also it  specifically highlights the role of  perfectionism in weight loss journeys  i'm really glad that will has a skilled  team here who genuinely seems to have  his best interest at heart but i also  know that most people looking to lose  weight do not have those same supports  this can and often is a slippery slope i  know because i was there and it resulted  in a pretty severe ed so i acknowledge  that for a lot of you weight loss is  very much desired but this is just my  reminder to really know your triggers  weigh out the benefits and risks of  dieting and to focus on more gentle  nutrition if dieting is not supportive  for you and if this is not triggering  please check out my video on gentle  nutrition tips for weight loss which you  can see right here and on that note that  is all that i have for you guys today if  you like this video be sure to give it  the thumbs up leave me a comment below  on your thoughts on the will smith  documentary thank you again to billbar  for sponsoring subscribe and i will see  you next time on abby's kitchen bye  [Music]

